WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:08.480
Welcome and thank you for taking the
time to review FORAP. FORAP stands for

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.800
framework for organizing reusable and
adaptable project-based learning

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.880
projects. It is designed specifically
for computing education where

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:22.080
project-based learning can be powerful,
but where projects can also be difficult

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:26.800
to design, adopt, adapt, and implement
across different course contexts. The

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.400
purpose of this short presentation is to
give you a guided overview of the

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.320
framework before you review the full
website and complete the questionnaire.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.160
The website is the main reference
material. This presentation is only an

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:43.200
entry point to help explain the
motivation and structure of the framework

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:48.719
and how its parts fit together.
Before looking at FORAP itself, it is useful

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:53.120
to briefly situate it in project-based
learning in computing.

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:57.680
In computing courses, project-based
learning often asks students to work

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:02.800
through realistic and meaningful project
tasks. Depending on the project,

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:08.720
students may need to plan, research,
design, implement, test, debug,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:13.040
document, deploy, and present their
work. They may also need to collaborate

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:17.920
with teammates, make technical
decisions, work through ambiguity, and

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:22.880
connect course concepts to a larger
computing problem.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:28.479
FORAP does not try to define all of
project-based learning. Instead, it

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:33.119
focuses on how computing PjBL projects
can be organized and packaged so that

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:38.320
they can be reused and adapted by other
instructors across different courses and

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:43.920
instructional contexts. FORAP is
motivated by three recurring imperatives

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:49.040
in computing project-based learning. The
first is the instructor imperative.

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:53.840
Instructors often face substantial
workload when they design a new project,

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:58.079
adopt an existing project, adapt that
project to a local course context,

00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:03.759
implement it during the semester, and
sustain the materials over time. The

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.360
second is the student imperative.
Computing projects often involve

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.840
technical barriers that can prevent
students from making progress. These

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:15.840
barriers can include installation and
configuration problems, gaps in

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:20.800
prerequisite knowledge, debugging
difficulties, testing, deployment,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.879
version control, documentation, and
other practical challenges that arise

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:29.360
during project work.
The third is the assessment imperative.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.680
In project-based learning, it can be
difficult to assess higher-order

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:37.760
thinking, formative progress, individual
contribution in collaborative settings

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:42.959
and what students have actually learned
beyond the final project artifact.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.720
FORAP is intended to respond to these
imperatives by organizing project

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:53.519
materials in a more coordinated and
adoption-ready way. So what is FORAP?

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:58.319
FORAP is a packaging framework for
computing PjBL projects. It is a way to

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:03.920
organize a project so that another
instructor can understand it, judge its

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:08.959
fit, adopt it, and adapt it. The
framework includes principles, project

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:13.120
design goals, and structural components.
Together, these help define what should

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:19.360
be included in a reusable project
package. It is also important to say

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:25.680
what FORAP is not. FORAP is not a single
project. It is not a full curriculum. It

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:30.239
is not a general PjBL process framework.
It is focused on computing PjBL projects

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.799
and on how those projects can be
packaged with coordinated support and

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:39.360
assessment materials. The architecture
of FORAP gives the framework its

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:44.799
overall structure. At the top are the
guiding principles. These include two

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:48.959
groups, the PjBL principles and the
design goals. The principles describe

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.959
the pedagogical direction of the
framework while the design goals

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:57.840
translate the needs of instructors and
students into practical directions for

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:02.720
organizing projects. The middle of the
architecture is the set of support

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:07.200
packages. FORAP organizes each project
through three complementary packages. An

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:12.239
instructor support package, a student
support package, and an assessment

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:17.040
package. The PjBL principles and design
goals motivate and guide how these

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.959
support packages are organized. The
architecture also includes project

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:25.600
attributes which help describe the
nature and fit of a project and a

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:30.720
project portfolio which is intended to
support discovery and sharing of fully

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:35.520
packaged FORAP projects. The project
attributes provide the representation of

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:39.840
each project within the portfolio.
The next slides walk through these parts

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:44.320
of the architecture in order. The first
major part of the architecture is the

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:49.919
guiding principles. The guiding
principles include two groups. The first

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:53.919
group is the PjBL principles. These are
principles of project-based learning

00:04:51.759 --> 00:04:59.440
that are especially relevant to project
design in computing.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:03.919
The five PjBL principles are authentic
learning, active learning, self-directed

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:08.800
learning, collaborative learning, and
critical thinking. In FORAP, authentic

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:13.120
learning means that projects should
connect learning to realistic and

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:17.919
relevant contexts. Active and
self-directed learning emphasize student

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:23.120
engagement in project tasks, planning,
research, decision-making, and working through

00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:27.039
ambiguity with appropriate support.
Collaborative learning emphasizes

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:31.520
teamwork, communication, and shared
responsibility. As for the support

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:36.160
packages, the first one is the
instructor support package. This package

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:41.919
includes the materials an instructor
needs in order to adopt, implement, and

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:47.039
adapt a project with less effort. It is
intended to reduce barriers related to

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:51.280
time, planning, organization, and
student support.

00:05:49.039 --> 00:05:55.440
The project overview explains what the
project is for and summarizes its

00:05:53.520 --> 00:06:00.479
learning objectives, prerequisites,
knowledge, nature of tasks, and key

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:05.919
project attributes. It also provides
adoption and adaptation guidance.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.160
Instructor notes document practical
implementation knowledge. This can

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:14.800
include teaching notes, common student
difficulties, points where students

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:18.560
usually need help, timing suggestions,
and what needs updates as technologies

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.800
change.
The package can also include a reference

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:28.319
course, which shows how the project has
been integrated into a syllabus, course

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:32.479
schedule, and assessment plan.
The package also includes sample task

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:37.199
solutions that support instructor
preparation, demonstration, and

00:06:34.479 --> 00:06:41.840
evaluation. The second support package
is the student support package. This

00:06:39.919 --> 00:06:45.919
package contains the materials students
use to make progress during the project.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:50.400
It is designed to address common
technical challenges, especially

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:54.160
installation and configuration issues,
prerequisite gaps, and difficulties

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:58.720
students encounter while completing
project tasks. The package includes

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:03.120
installation guidelines, which may include
written or video guides, or tools that help

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:07.759
students set up required tools and
environments. It includes task

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:13.039
scaffolding such as task instructions,
milestone checklists, starter code,

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:17.360
configuration files, hints, diagrams,
and templates. It includes laboratory

00:07:15.599 --> 00:07:22.000
exercises that help students fill
prerequisite knowledge gaps or practice

00:07:19.599 --> 00:07:26.160
required tools. It also includes
resources such as lecture slides,

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:29.840
official documentation, protocol
specifications, or other supporting

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:34.880
references students may need during
the project. The main purpose is to

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.960
reduce avoidable technical barriers and
help students continue making progress

00:07:36.639 --> 00:07:43.919
through the project. The third support
package is the assessment package. This

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:49.120
package includes materials used to
support both formative and summative

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:53.199
assessment in a FORAP project. It is
organized around the project's learning

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:58.080
objectives so that assessment remains
connected to what students are expected

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:02.639
to know and be able to do.
Phased milestones define intermediate

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:07.599
deliverables and feedback points during
project execution. These milestones help

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:12.879
make student progress visible while the
project is still unfolding.

00:08:10.479 --> 00:08:16.800
Transfer-oriented design problems are
scenario-based problems that assess

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:21.599
learning beyond the original project
tasks. They are intended to elicit

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:25.360
higher-order thinking and can be used in
exams, presentations, or project

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:29.840
reviews.
The detailed rubric provides criteria

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:34.560
aligned with the learning objectives for
evaluating project artifacts such as

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:38.399
code, design documents, reports, and
presentations.

00:08:36.399 --> 00:08:42.479
Together, these assessment materials
help connect the project work to

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:47.200
evidence of learning. In addition to the
support packages, FORAP describes each

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:52.480
project through a set of project
attributes. These attributes act as a

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:57.120
concise project profile. They help
instructors understand the nature of the

00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:02.560
project, compare it with other projects,
judge whether it fits a course, and

00:08:59.279 --> 00:09:06.560
identify where adaptation may be needed.
The first describes whether the project

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:11.680
is a greenfield project built from
scratch or a brownfield project that

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:15.600
modifies an existing system.
The second attribute is scope and

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:20.480
duration which describes the time
required for completion.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:24.399
The third is technical complexity which
describes the expected level of

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:28.080
difficulty in terms of technical depth,
integration effort and implementation

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:32.000
demands.
The fourth attribute is domain or

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:36.080
discipline. This identifies the
computing area or subject context of the

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:40.399
project.
The fifth attribute is competency

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:44.560
specification which is optional. When
included, it makes the project's

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:48.480
learning objectives explicit. It can be
written in a clear, structured, and

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:53.040
comparable format such as through the
competency model of CC2020.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:58.080
An illustrative example is the
Touchalytics project. In this project,

00:09:55.839 --> 00:10:03.040
students build an Android authentication
application that uses swipe behavior as

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:06.959
a biometric signal. The project involves
storing interaction data in cloud

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:11.600
storage and processing that data with a
Python-based machine learning backend.

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:16.320
The example shows how a project can be
described using the FORAP attributes.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:21.360
The codebase context is a greenfield
mobile application project. The scope is

00:10:18.959 --> 00:10:25.760
medium to large, typically 6 to 8 weeks
with staged milestones. The technical

00:10:23.839 --> 00:10:30.079
complexity is high because the project
involves mobile development, behavioral

00:10:27.839 --> 00:10:34.000
data capture, cloud storage, and machine
learning-based authentication. The

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:38.160
domain includes mobile computing,
behavioral biometrics, and machine

00:10:35.839 --> 00:10:41.600
learning. The competency statement is to
design a mobile behavioral-authentication

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:46.240
application that captures and stores
swipe interactions and applies machine

00:10:43.519 --> 00:10:50.160
learning. The example also shows how the
support packages can be applied. Student

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:54.959
materials include setup guidance for
Python, Android Studio, Firebase, and

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:58.399
machine learning libraries. Instructor
materials include adoption guidance,

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:02.160
deployment notes, implementation
samples, and sample solutions.

00:11:00.399 --> 00:11:06.640
Assessment materials include staged
milestones, rubrics, and transfer-oriented

00:11:04.079 --> 00:11:10.800
design problems. The project
portfolio is intended to become a

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:16.720
centralized place where computing
instructors can find, compare, and share

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:21.040
FORAP-based projects. The website
currently shows a collection of packaged

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:25.279
projects in the portfolio. These
projects represent different computing

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:28.959
areas, greenfield and brownfield
contexts, difficulty levels, and

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:33.600
durations.
The longer-term direction is to turn this

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:38.320
collection into a public platform where
instructors can share packaged projects,

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:44.079
find projects from others, and search or
filter by FORAP attributes. This

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:47.839
concludes the brief overview of FORAP.
Please use the FORAP website as the

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:51.839
reference material for the full
framework details.

00:11:49.839 --> 00:11:56.480
As a next step, we would like to
validate the framework by collecting

00:11:53.680 --> 00:12:00.160
feedback from reviewers. After reviewing
the material, please complete the

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:05.440
questionnaire which is intended to
collect feedback on the clarity,

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:10.399
completeness, and usefulness of FORAP.
Thank you again for reviewing the

00:12:06.959 --> 00:12:10.399
framework and providing your feedback.
